<div class="yarbo-rich-content" style="max-width: 900px; margin: 48px auto; padding: 0 20px; line-height: 1.7; color: #393939;">

  <p style="letter-spacing:2px;font-size:0.8em;color:#c9a84c;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;"><strong>The Yarbo System, Explained</strong></p>
  <h2 style="color:#232739;font-size:1.8em;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:20px;">One Robot, Built to Run Every Season of the Year</h2>

  <p>Most yard equipment does one job for a few weeks a year and sits idle the rest of the time. A mower cuts grass from spring through fall, then spends winter in a shed. A snow blower does the opposite, four or five months of use followed by seven or eight months of storage. Yarbo was built around a different idea: one tracked, all-terrain base, called the Core, that stays working all year by swapping the tool on the front. Mow in summer. Blow leaves in fall. Clear snow in winter. Same machine, same navigation, same app, different attachment.</p>

  <p>That single decision, building a base platform instead of three separate machines, is why Yarbo owners describe it less like buying a mower and more like installing infrastructure. You're not replacing a lawn mower. You're putting a year-round autonomous maintenance system on the property, and adding capability to it as your needs, and your budget, allow. The navigation hardware, drive motors, and battery interface that make up the most expensive part of any robotic outdoor equipment get used twelve months a year instead of four.</p>

  <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0965/2056/9136/files/yarbo-core--pos1--Yarbo_Core_7.webp?v=1790046217" alt="Yarbo Core tracked robotic base" style="width:100%; border-radius:8px; margin: 24px 0;" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024">

  <h3 style="color:#232739;margin-top:36px;">How the Modular System Actually Works</h3>

  <p>The Yarbo Core is the constant. It's a tracked, all-terrain chassis with the navigation computer, drive motors, battery interface, and connectivity built in. On its own, the Core does nothing, it's designed that way on purpose. Every capability comes from the module bolted to the front: the Lawn Mower Pro Module, the Snow Blower Module, or the Leaf Blower Module. Swapping between them takes minutes, not tools and not a technician visit.</p>

  <p>This is different from how most robotic yard equipment is built. A typical robot mower is a single-purpose machine: buy it, use it five months a year, and its navigation hardware, drive system, and battery sit unused the other seven months. If you also want a robotic snow blower, you're buying that navigation stack a second time, and a third time for leaf cleanup, three separate purchases each carrying their own version of the most expensive components. Yarbo's Core carries that hardware across every season instead of duplicating it. The module you're not using simply sits in the garage, cheap to store, since the expensive parts stayed on the Core doing something else.</p>

  <h3 style="color:#232739;margin-top:36px;">Setup: An Afternoon, Not a Contractor Visit</h3>

  <p>There's no trenching crew, no buried wire to schedule, and no waiting for a technician appointment. You unbox the Core, download the Yarbo app, and walk the perimeter of your property once. The app builds a map from that walk, accurate to within a few centimeters. From there you draw zones directly on the map on your phone: the front lawn, the back lawn, the driveway, a no-go area around the garden beds. Each zone can carry its own settings, cutting height for one area, a gentler mowing pattern for another, a different snow-clearing route for the driveway versus the front walk.</p>

  <p>Because the map lives on your phone and in the Core, redrawing a zone later, say, after adding a new flower bed, takes a few minutes in the app. There's no wire to dig up and relocate. This is the single biggest practical difference between an RTK-based system like Yarbo and the older generation of boundary-wire robot mowers still common on the market: with a wire, changing your yard means changing the wire. With RTK and vision, changing your yard means updating a map.</p>

  <h3 style="color:#232739;margin-top:36px;">Navigation That Doesn't Need a Buried Wire</h3>

  <p>Every Yarbo Core navigates using RTK (Real-Time Kinematic) satellite positioning combined with binocular vision and inertial/odometry sensors, what Yarbo calls RTK + Vision + IMU + ODOM. In practical terms: it maps your property to centimeter-level accuracy using your phone, with no trenching, no boundary wire, and no beacons to install. You draw zones in the app, and the Core holds its line even under tree cover, where GPS-only systems typically drift, because the vision and inertial sensors keep tracking position even when satellite signal weakens for a moment.</p>

  <p>The Core's tracked drive system climbs slopes up to 70% grade (35 degrees), terrain that stops most wheeled robotic equipment outright, and it can pull Yarbo's tow attachments and trailer. A wheeled robot mower relies on traction and weight distribution that simply doesn't hold on a real hillside or loose gravel; tracks distribute weight across a much larger contact area and grip terrain that would spin a wheel uselessly. Connectivity runs over 4G, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and Wi-Fi HaLow, so the Core stays reachable across a large property even far from the house router, an acre or more from your Wi-Fi access point isn't unusual on the properties this system is built for.</p>

  <h3 style="color:#232739;margin-top:36px;">One App, Every Season</h3>

  <p>The Yarbo app is the same interface whether the Core is wearing the mower, the snow blower, or the leaf blower. You get one login, one set of zone maps, one schedule calendar, and one notification feed, regardless of which module is attached. Scheduling works the same way across all three: set the days and times you want it to run, and it runs, and the Lawn Mower Pro uses rain detection to react when the weather turns. When you swap modules for the season, the app recognizes the change and switches the relevant settings and safety parameters automatically, you don't rebuild your zone map from scratch each time.</p>

  <h3 style="color:#232739;margin-top:36px;">Built for Real Terrain, Not Just a Flat Suburban Lawn</h3>

  <p>A lot of robotic yard equipment is designed and tested on flat, obstacle-free lots. Yarbo's target customer typically has a full acre or more, often with slopes, gravel sections, tree roots, and outbuildings that most consumer robots simply aren't rated for. The Core's tracked drive and 70% (35°) climbing ability were engineered around that reality rather than around a demo-day flat lawn. With Yarbo's tow hitch and trailer it can also haul supplies around the property.</p>

  <hr style="border:none;border-top:1px solid #e5e5e5;margin:48px 0 0;">
  <h3 style="color:#232739;margin-top:48px;">The Lawn Mower Pro Module: Cuts What Other Robots Can't</h3>

  <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0965/2056/9136/files/Yarbo_M1_1.png?v=1789594137" alt="Yarbo Lawn Mower Pro Module attached to Core" style="width:100%; border-radius:8px; margin: 20px 0;" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024">

  <p>The Lawn Mower Pro Module turns the Core into a heavy-duty robotic mower built for large properties and demanding grass types. Dual 300W motors deliver up to 2,500W of peak power, and Yarbo rates it for both cool and warm season grasses, for residential and commercial lawns. Cutting width is 20 inches (500mm), with height adjustable from 0.8 to 4.0 inches, so you can run a tight, manicured cut on the main lawn and a taller, lower-maintenance height on rougher back-property sections, all from the same machine and the same zone map. Coverage is rated up to 6 acres a week on the Y40 and 7 acres on the Y40P, at two cuts a week.</p>

  <p>Obstacle avoidance combines a binocular camera, ultrasonic radar, and a physical bumper, three overlapping detection systems rather than relying on just one. That layered approach matters on a large property where the machine will inevitably encounter something the map didn't account for, a fallen branch, a pet, a child's toy left on the lawn, and needs to stop or route around it reliably every time, not most of the time.</p>

  <h3 style="color:#232739;margin-top:36px;">The Snow Blower Module: Clears the Driveway Before You're Awake</h3>

  <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0965/2056/9136/files/Snow_Blower_Module.webp?v=1789851770" alt="Yarbo Snow Blower Module clearing a driveway" style="width:100%; border-radius:8px; margin: 20px 0;" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024">

  <p>The Snow Blower Module is genuinely two-stage clearing, not a single-stage auger dressed up as one. A 24-inch clearing width and 12-inch intake height pull dry, wet, and packed snow through in a single pass. The chute throws snow 6 to 40 feet and rotates from -10&deg; to 190&deg;, with the deflector adjustable from -5&deg; to 50&deg;, so snow lands exactly where you want it instead of back on the driveway or into a neighbor's yard.</p>

  <p>It clears on the schedule you set in the Yarbo app and returns to its dock to recharge on its own. It covers up to 6,000 ft&sup2; per charge on the Y40 and 7,000 ft&sup2; on the Y40P at 1 inch of snow, works on paved, concrete and gravel surfaces, and is rated for slopes up to 36% (20&deg;). It is IPX5 rated and operates from -13&deg;F to 113&deg;F.</p>

  <h3 style="color:#232739;margin-top:36px;">The Leaf Blower Module: Fall Cleanup on Autopilot</h3>

  <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0965/2056/9136/files/Blower_Module1.webp?v=1789744660" alt="Yarbo Leaf Blower Module clearing leaves" style="width:100%; border-radius:8px; margin: 20px 0;" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024">

  <p>The Leaf Blower Module clears leaves and yard debris on a repeatable schedule you set once. It works lawns, paths, and hard surfaces automatically through the entire fall season, the season most homeowners describe as the single worst stretch of yard work all year, without you spending a single one of those weekends outside with a handheld blower or a bagged-leaves trip to the curb every few days. It moves 174 mph air at 607 CFM and works on slopes up to 70% (35&deg;).</p>

